#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SESSION_NOTES-S235-through-S232.md.verify.sh — the losslessness proof for this shard.
#
# Generated by Session 239 (the SIXTH trim). Re-runnable, read-only, exits non-zero on failure.
#
#   bash <this>              prove the sixth trim was lossless AND landed where it was declared
#   bash <this> --self-test  prove this proof can FAIL (68 mutants; exit 2 if any survives)
#
# Descended from Session 235's SESSION_NOTES-S231-through-S228.md.verify.sh, itself descended from
# Session 231's, Session 228's, Session 224's and Session 222's — read those for the rationale
# behind L0-L3, L4, L5-L7, L8-L9 and L10-L11. Shards are WRITE-ONCE: this proof pins ITS OWN cut key
# and does not re-prove the earlier shards' cuts. Run all six.
#
# NOTHING INHERITED IS NARROWED OR WEAKENED HERE. The fifth trim narrowed L2/b3 to the banner and
# attached a condition to that narrowing: b3 is safe in the banner-only form ONLY while TRANSFORM
# is None and L1 is present and evaluated. Both hold at this cut (TRANSFORM is None below; L1 runs
# in check()), so the narrowed form is carried forward unchanged. A trim that ever needs a rebase
# must restore b3's records half in the same commit.
#
# ONE ASSERTION IS NEW HERE, and the hole it closes was this lineage's own.
#
#   L12 (ARITHMETIC) — every number a trim states about the SIZE of its own artifacts, derived from
#      those artifacts and held against the prose that states it.
#
#      THE HOLE WAS MEASURED, NOT SUSPECTED. The fifth trim's pointer block says "4 record headings,
#      918 lines"; its banner says "At 976 lines it sits under the 2,000-line agent read cap";
#      BACKLOG.md says "(976 lines)". All three are CORRECT — re-measured while writing this file:
#      that shard is 976 lines, 918 of them records, 4 headings. But grep its proof for either
#      number and every hit is inside a string literal: POINTER, BANNER, and one L8 required string.
#      L2/b1 pins the pointer against a declaration the same author wrote; L6 pins the banner the
#      same way; L8 checks that BACKLOG.md contains the declared characters. Not one of them
#      compares a figure to the artifact. A trim that typed 918 where the truth was 981 would have
#      shipped that in three files at once, green.
#
#      IT IS THE FAILURE CLASS THIS PROJECT REPORTS MOST. Five consecutive sessions before this one
#      self-assessed a numeral typed instead of derived (PROJECT_LEARNINGS #105, #146, #148, #152).
#      A proof lineage that mechanises byte identity to twelve assertions and leaves its own
#      arithmetic to prose has automated the part it was already good at.
#
#      TWO INDEPENDENT HALVES, the same shape L11 uses. The NUMERIC half measures the artifacts and
#      holds them against hand-declared integers (SIZES). The PROSE half holds those integers
#      against the formatted figures that actually appear in the live pointer block, the shard's
#      banner, README.md, BACKLOG.md and CLAUDE.md — ten claims across five texts, in two formats,
#      because this project's prose writes small counts as words ("four record headings") and large
#      ones as digits ("1,057 lines"). Declaring the integers and the prose separately is what keeps
#      each arm independently reachable; deriving one from the other would collapse nine mutants
#      into one.
#
#      ONE NUMBER IN SIZES IS DECLARED AND NOT MEASURED, and saying so is part of the assertion.
#      READ_CAP (2,000) is a property of the agent harness, not of any file in this repository;
#      nothing here can measure it. L12/cap therefore asserts the SHARD is under the declared cap —
#      which is the claim the banner actually makes — and never that the cap itself is right.
#
#      THE MUTANT COUNT IS CHECKED THE SAME WAY, and it is not decoration: the banner tells a reader
#      how thorough --self-test is, and a reader who believes "67" while the file ships 12 has been
#      misled by exactly the mechanism L12 exists to stop. SIZES carries the declared count, the
#      prose half finds it in the banner, and the self-test asserts it equals len(mutants).
#
#      THIS ASSERTION CAUGHT ITS OWN AUTHOR TWICE BEFORE IT SHIPPED, which is the only reason
#      to trust it. The first green run printed "10 prose claims" a few lines under a header
#      that said nine, and the arm loop shipped `retained_records` with no mutant of its own.
#      A typed count and an unreachable arm — the two defects this file exists to make
#      impossible — both in the file that introduces the fix. Neither was reasoned out: the
#      run printed the first and the neuter loop printed the second.
#
# ONE ASSERTION WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED, and the measurement is why. CLAUDE.md, this file's
# banner and the pointer block all state in bold that no shard name is a prefix of another, and
# nothing checks it. An L13 was drafted. It was dropped because NO MUTANT CAN REACH IT: every
# rename that creates a prefix or substring relation either points a routing clause at a file that
# cannot be read (L5/4 fires) or points two clauses at one file whose ids then fall outside one of
# their ranges (L5/4 again), and a rename that touches only the declaration is caught by L8/set.
# Shipping an assertion no mutant reaches is the exact defect this lineage has now found three
# times (L2/b0 in the second trim, L5/2 in the third, four more arms in an adversarial sweep of the
# fourth). It is recorded here so the seventh trim does not rediscover the idea and ship it.
#
# MEASURED COVERAGE — every line below was produced by running the loops, not by predicting them.
# Neuter one assertion at a time and re-run --self-test; what survives is what it ALONE catches:
#
#     for A in L0 L1 L2 L3 L4 L5 L6 L7 L8 L9 L10 L11 L12; do
#       sed "s|^    fails += $A(.*|    pass|" <this> > /tmp/n.sh
#       echo "$A: $(bash /tmp/n.sh --self-test | grep SURVIVED)"; done
#
#     L0 (none)   L1 (none)*  L2 M10,M12-M15,M40,M51   L3 (none)   L4 (none)   L5 M18-M26,
#     M38,M39,M47,M52   L6 M27   L7 M29,M30   L8 M31-M34,M41,M43-M46,M50   L9 M35-M37,M48
#     L10 M53-M55   L11 M56-M59   L12 M60-M68
#
#     * L1's blank entry is NOT a licence to remove it. L3 and L4 happen to catch every mutant L1
#       catches, so nothing depends on L1 ALONE — but L1 is the assertion the fifth trim's
#       narrowing of L2/b3 rests on, and b3's records half is dead only while L1 runs and
#       TRANSFORM is None. Read '(none)' here as 'overlapped', never as 'removable'.
#
# THAT LOOP IS NOT ENOUGH, and this lineage has proved it four times now: the SECOND trim shipped
# L2/b0 reachable by no mutant, the THIRD shipped L5/2's partition arm, an adversarial sweep of the
# FOURTH found four more — and at THIS trim the arm sweep below found two of its own, both in the
# assertion this trim introduces. An assertion is a handful of separate `if`s; neutering the whole
# function cannot tell you that one of those `if`s has never fired. So neuter one `if` at a time as
# well. All 42 arms were swept that way here. The mutant each arm alone catches:
#
#     L2/b3 M51          L5/1 M18-M23,M26     L5/2 shape M47      L5/2 loop M39
#     L5/4 stray M25     L5/4 unread M52      L8/required M50     L8/forbidden M44
#     L8/set M45,M46     L9/no-add M48        L9/missing M36      L9/differs M35,M37
#     L10/no-freeze M53  L10/missing M54      L10/differs M55     L11/fire M56
#     L11/target M57     L11/floor M58        L11/declared M59
#     L12/numeric M60,M61,M62,M68             L12/cap M63
#     L12/prose-literal M65,M66               L12/prose-figure M64,M67
#
# TWO OF THIS TRIM'S OWN ARMS FAILED THAT SWEEP ON THE FIRST TRY, and the fix was a mutant both
# times, never a weakened assertion:
#   * L2/b3 LOST ITS ONLY MUTANT TO L12. The fifth trim's M51 APPENDS a front-matter line to the
#     banner, which under this proof also changes the shard's line count — so L12/shard_total
#     caught it and b3 was left unreachable. M51 now REPLACES a banner line instead, keeping the
#     count identical. A narrowed assertion whose one mutant is stolen by a NEW assertion is
#     retired in silence; that is how an arm dies, and only the loop can tell you it happened.
#   * L12/cap HAD NO MUTANT AT ALL. Moving the declared cap alone also falsifies the banner's
#     prose figure, so L12/prose caught it first. M63 now mis-states the cap CONSISTENTLY —
#     declaration, banner and declared literal together — which is both the isolating mutation and
#     the realistic one.
#
# TWELVE ARMS HAVE NO UNIQUELY-CATCHING MUTANT, and saying which — and why — is more useful than
# inventing contrived ones. This list is MEASURED; do not trust it after editing anything:
#
#   * L0/empty — no artifact mutation can reach it. Emptying a records zone moves bytes, so L1, L3
#     and L4 fire first. It guards a GRAMMAR break, which is not expressible as a mutation of the
#     artifacts. It is a guard rail, and that is the honest name.
#   * L2/b0 — M14, M15 and M40 fire it, but each also breaks b1 and b2, so none depends on it.
#     Kept anyway, because b0 NAMES the cause where b1 only shows bytes. (The fourth trim's header
#     claimed a unique mutant for it; that was measurably false and the fifth trim said so.)
#   * L2/pointer, L2/b1, L2/b2 — mutually covering. M8 trips pointer and L5/1; M10, M12-M15 and
#     M40 trip b1 and b2 together.
#   * L3/MISSING, L3/ADDED, L3/ORDER — subsumed by L1 and L4 on every mutation available here.
#   * L4/retained, L4/archived — subsumed by L5/4 since the third trim added it, as that trim said.
#     Kept: L4 states the cut key directly instead of inferring it, and names both sides on failure.
#   * L5/2 final junction — STRUCTURALLY subsumed: it is the junction between the shard clause and
#     the live clause, and L5/3 pins both exactly, so no single-clause mutation can reach it.
#   * L5/3 shard, L5/3 live — M24 also trips L5/4.
#   * L5/4 vacuous — mutually covering with L5/4 unreadable for a None result.
#   * L8/unreadable and L12/prose-unreadable — MUTUALLY COVERING ACROSS TWO ASSERTIONS, which is
#     weaker than within one, and no mutation available here can separate them: M49 makes
#     README.md unreadable and both fire. L12's only nullable texts ARE L8's four files — its
#     other two, the live pointer and the shard banner, come from the artifacts and can never be
#     None. So the pair is honestly unseparable rather than merely unmutated. If a future trim
#     gives L12 a text L8 does not read, that stops being true and this arm needs its own mutant.
#
# The grammar is FROZEN into this file on purpose: a later change to the trim design must not
# silently change what THIS shard's proof means.
